{:adr-id "2629500000"
 :title "cloud-itonami ISIC 5011 (sea and coastal passenger water transport) FerryOperationsAdvisor + Maritime Safety Governor actor"
 :status :accepted
 :date "2026-07-16"
 :deciders ["Jun Kawasaki (agent-executed, standing authorization)"]
 :related ["ADR-2607102400" "ADR-2607141600" "ADR-2607011000"]
 :mirrors "cloud-itonami-isic-5020 (tanker.* module shape, nearest transport-sector sibling) + cloud-itonami-isic-6511 (current-gen langchain-store + portable .cljc test convention, reference adopter)"

 :context
 "ISIC Rev.5 5011 = sea and coastal passenger water transport. Live
  registry.edn :name for \"5011\" independently verified fresh (cloned
  kotoba-lang/industry read-only): \"Sea and coastal passenger water
  transport\", exact match. Unlike most cloud-itonami-isic-* targets in
  this fleet, this was NOT a 404 target -- cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-
  isic-5011 already existed as a legitimate :blueprint-tier scaffold,
  published by ADR-2607102400 (created+pushed 2026-07-09) with
  boilerplate docs (CODE_OF_CONDUCT/
  CONTRIBUTING/GOVERNANCE/LICENSE/README/SECURITY/blueprint.edn/docs)
  but no deps.edn/src/test. blueprint.edn already declared
  :itonami.blueprint/governor :maritime-safety-governor and README
  already named a 'Ferry Operations Advisor -> Maritime Safety
  Governor' contract -- confirmed via `gh api
  repos/cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-isic-5011` before starting, per
  this task's premise-verification requirement. This ADR records
  filling that scaffold in, not creating a fresh repo.
  cloud-itonami-isic-4911 (passenger rail), the originally suggested
  mirror target, was itself still :blueprint-tier (being built
  concurrently by a sibling agent in this same batch), so
  cloud-itonami-isic-5020 (water freight/tanker, :implemented) and
  cloud-itonami-isic-6511 (life insurance, :implemented, pushed
  2026-07-14, the kotoba-lang/langchain-store reference adopter) were
  used as the mirrored references instead. Passenger ferry service has
  a direct passenger-safety dimension (maritime) -- per this batch's
  Wave 2 (coordination/logistics) guardrail, the closed op allowlist
  must never include an op that directly finalizes a maritime-safety-
  authority decision; any 'flag a concern' op always escalates, never
  auto-commit-eligible."

 :scope-exclusions
 ["finalizing a sail-clearance decision (clearing a vessel to sail after a reported seaworthiness fault) -- hard, permanent block"
  "overriding a weather hold -- hard, permanent block"
  "authorizing departure despite a reported fault -- hard, permanent block"
  "direct vessel control / real dispatch of any kind -- not represented in the closed op allowlist at all; every proposal's :effect is HARD-required to be literally :propose"]

 :self-trip-fix
 "Known bug class this fleet has repeatedly hit and independently fixed
  across multiple sibling actors: a governor's scope-exclusion term list
  phrased as a bare noun (e.g. 'safety', 'clearance', 'weather-hold')
  accidentally matches inside the mock advisor's own DEFAULT
  rationale/disclaimer text for a legitimate, allowed proposal --
  especially acute here since :schedule-sailing-operation's own
  rationale, when the sailing has a reported fault or active weather
  hold, necessarily discusses those exact conditions, and
  :flag-maritime-safety-concern's entire purpose is to surface them.
  Fix: ferry.governor/scope-exclusion-phrases is phrased as full
  finalization/execution ACTIONS ('finalize the sail-clearance
  override', 'override the weather-hold', 'clear the vessel to sail
  despite the fault', 'authorize departure despite the reported
  fault'), never a bare noun -- and ferry.ferryadvisor's own
  rationale/disclaimer text is written in DIFFERENT words than any of
  those phrases (attributing final departure-clearance judgment to
  'the vessel master and the maritime-safety authority' rather than
  restating what the actor itself does not do, so no negation of the
  exact trigger phrase appears either). Verified via a dedicated
  regression test, default-mock-advisor-proposals-never-self-trip-
  scope-exclusion in test/ferry/scope_exclusion_test.cljc, sweeping
  every op (:log-voyage-record/:schedule-sailing-operation/
  :flag-maritime-safety-concern/:coordinate-maintenance) x every demo
  sailing (including sail-6, the fault-reported + weather-hold-active
  one) through the DEFAULT mock advisor and asserting none of the
  resulting proposals trip :scope-exclusion-sail-clearance. A separate
  direct-unit-test file, test/ferry/governor_test.cljc, proves the
  check is not vacuous: a hand-crafted proposal containing each phrase
  verbatim IS hard-blocked."

 :decision
 "Fill in the pre-existing cloud-itonami-isic-5011 :blueprint scaffold
  with a real, tested FerryOperationsAdvisor + Maritime Safety Governor
  actor, mirroring cloud-itonami-isic-5020's tanker.* module shape as
  ferry.*:
  - Store (ferry.store): Store protocol + MemStore + DatomicStore, the
    latter a kotoba-lang/langchain-store field-spec entity-store
    adopter (ADR-2607141600, NOT hand-rolled enc/dec -- this is a NEW
    store as of 2026-07). sailing entity (vessel-imo/jurisdiction/
    certification-verified?/certification-record-id/seaworthiness-
    fault-reported?/weather-hold-active?/scheduled?/maintenance-
    coordinated?), append-only ledger + schedule/concern/maintenance
    history, MemStore<->DatomicStore contract parity proven.
  - Facts (ferry.facts): per-jurisdiction passenger-vessel safety-
    certification catalog (JPN/USA/GBR/NOR), real citations (MLIT/
    USCG/MCA/NMA, SOLAS Chapter III life-saving appliances), honest
    coverage reporting -- deliberately SEPARATE regime from
    cloud-itonami-isic-5020's cargo-vessel (SOLAS Chapter II-2) facts.
  - Registry (ferry.registry): draft record construction (sailing-
    schedule/concern-filing/maintenance-coordination, all unsigned
    drafts) + imo-number-valid? (honest reapplication of
    cloud-itonami-isic-5020's SOLAS/IMO A.600(15) 7-digit check-digit
    scheme -- applies to every SOLAS-class ship, not only tankers).
  - Advisor (ferry.ferryadvisor): Advisor protocol + mock-advisor +
    llm-advisor, 4 ops all :effect :propose -- log-voyage-record,
    schedule-sailing-operation, flag-maritime-safety-concern,
    coordinate-maintenance.
  - Governor (ferry.governor, the Maritime Safety Governor): 7 named
    HARD checks in priority order (op-not-allowed, effect-not-propose,
    scope-exclusion-sail-clearance [permanent], certification-
    incomplete, imo-number-invalid, no-spec-basis, already-scheduled)
    + high-stakes gate #{:flag-maritime-safety-concern} + confidence
    floor 0.6.
  - Phase (ferry.phase): 0->3 rollout (phase1 = log-voyage-record only,
    approval; phase2 += schedule-sailing-operation, approval; phase3
    writes all 4 ops but auto-commits ONLY log-voyage-record;
    flag-maritime-safety-concern never auto, any phase, AND not
    writable until phase 3).
  - Operation (ferry.operation): real langgraph-clj StateGraph
    (intake->advise->govern->decide->commit|hold|request-approval),
    interrupt-before #{:request-approval}, NOT a stub -- mirrors
    tanker.operation/underwriting.operation exactly. commit-record
    dispatch keyed on the request's :op (not the proposal's :effect,
    which is a constant :propose by design) -- documented explicitly
    as a deliberate deviation from the mirrored siblings.
  - Sim (ferry.sim): clojure -M:dev:run demo, happy paths + always-
    escalate flow + a low-confidence-but-clean escalation + every
    HARD-hold scenario.
  - Tests, all .cljc (not .clj, per this workspace's cljs-first
    mandate): facts_test, registry_test, phase_test, governor_test
    (direct unit tests of op-not-allowed/effect-not-propose/scope-
    exclusion, incl. the mandatory self-trip regression date),
    governor_contract_test (full-graph flows), store_contract_test,
    scope_exclusion_test (the dedicated CRITICAL regression), plus a
    portable-cljs-test-runner + :cljs deps.edn alias mirroring
    cloud-itonami-isic-6511's current-generation convention.
  - All .cljc portable, no JVM-only interop. AGPL-3.0-or-later.
    Existing boilerplate docs (README/GOVERNANCE/CONTRIBUTING/
    SECURITY/CODE_OF_CONDUCT/blueprint.edn/docs/business-model.md/
    docs/operator-guide.md) were KEPT, not recreated; README.md was
    extended (not replaced) with R0/Run/Layout/Maturity sections; a
    docs/adr/0001-architecture.md child-repo ADR was added."

