  :incident-note "Honesty note (no failure hidden): the FIRST attempt to PUT the corrected industry_test.clj (commit 83a1aedbc040f09599e70899a2cf8ea0b96e82f0) accidentally sent EMPTY content -- a local `base64 <file>` invocation silently failed on this macOS's BSD base64 (which requires `-i <file>`, not a bare filename argument) and the script proceeded to PUT the resulting empty output without an explicit non-empty-content guard. This zeroed the live shared test file for well under a minute. Caught immediately by an unconditional post-PUT re-fetch-and-diff verification (this session's own standing practice, not a one-off), and corrected within the same turn by a second Contents-API PUT (commit dbdcec1107af99aad51d3b0a037f1f7c638ad79d) restoring the exact intended content, re-verified byte-for-byte identical to the locally-tested version afterward, then re-verified again via a brand-new fresh clone (`clojure -M:test`: 15 tests / 1061 assertions, 0 failures/0 errors; `clojure -M:lint`: 0 errors/0 warnings; no mojibake; 5011 entry and sample entries 3512/5020/5221 all intact). No other concurrent sibling PUT raced into the empty window. Lesson for future agents: always pass an explicit `-i <file> -o <file>` (or pipe via stdin) to `base64` on macOS, and always verify a PUT payload's `content` field is non-empty (e.g. assert base64 length > some floor) before sending, not only after."}}
